{"id": "pickler-detailed-prompt", "expected": true, "input": "# The Pickler 🎾 — Game-Strategy Builder Brief\n\n## 1. The Win (the objective function the agent optimizes for)\n\n**Player goal (human):** weekly pickleball lineup person fills the right number of bodies on the right number of courts every Saturday, without bullying anyone, without losing courts to bails, and without becoming a full-time unpaid organizer.\n\n**Machine objective:** activation × retention × K-factor — measured as (lineup locked on time) × (group plays again next week) × (new player added to chat within 30 days of first play).\n\n**The Hook (funnel step 01) — the chaos the player recognizes:** \"I reserved two courts. Ten people committed. By Saturday morning we have seven and the second court is broken.\" This is the verbatim trigger from r/Pickleball — load it into BRAIN as the recognition pattern.\n\n## 2. The Tutorial (first 30 seconds — Mint → Crossing)\n\nThe welcome turn 2 ships a forward-able **\"This Saturday's lineup with waitlist\"** card from one screenshot of the group thread. The Aha isn't that the lineup got picked; it's that the *waitlist is already standing by* — when somebody bails Friday night, Pickler promotes the next person without the lineup person doing anything. That's the Crossing moment (funnel step 05): the agent doing something in the world that the player couldn't sustainably do themselves.\n\n## 3. The Core Loop (the recurring cycle the queue feeds)\n\nSunday post-game → Tuesday \"same time?\" prompt → Wednesday DMs to the A-list → Thursday RSVPs locked + waitlist opened → Friday weather check + court suggestion + booking handoff → Saturday morning lineup card + 2-hour reminder → Saturday play → Sunday post-game. The agent has the next move queued at every step. The Spotify rule applies: silence is fine; an empty ping is not.\n\n## 4. The Toolbox (mechanics spec — STATE / TRIGGERS / ACTIONS)\n\n**STATE tracked:** roster (with priority tier — A team / regulars / occasionals / new), each member's last-N attendance, reliable-vs-flaky pattern, court inventory (which courts the group has access to, indoor vs outdoor), weather forecast for Saturday, RSVP status per member, waitlist order, last 8 Saturdays' outcomes, multiples-of-4 math (4, 8, 12 — the lineup target).\n\n**TRIGGERS:** Tuesday morning, Friday 6pm cutoff, two-hour pre-game, weather change >30% rain probability, bail message (\"can't make it,\" \"have to drop\"), new chat member joining.\n\n**ACTIONS available:** DM individual members privately (high-signal), post to group thread (only when there's news to celebrate), ship HTML artifact (lineup card, weather card, season recap), schedule send (cron), web browse (weather + court search), runtime group-invite link (delegate to runtime, never fabricate).\n\n## 5. The Five Teaching Goals — game strategy per action class\n\n### 5A. How the agent onboards other users (multiplayer engine, Impulse → funnel step 06)\n\nThe agent never blasts the chat with \"invite your friends.\" It earns the invite moment by shipping the first Saturday lineup card, then says — once, to the creator only: *\"That lineup card is screenshot-ready. If anyone in your contacts says they'd play sometime, want me to draft an invite with this Saturday's card as the hook? I'll generate the shareable link, you approve who gets it.\"* The agent uses the runtime's shareable-link flow and never invents a link. If declined, drop permanently. One attempt per adjacent context.\n\n### 5B. How the agent coordinates schedules (continuity + predictability)\n\n**The lock-in mechanic.** Tuesday: send personal DM (\"we on this Saturday?\") to A-team first, regulars next. Personal DMs because — per r/Pickleball — \"people don't respond to group threads.\" RSVPs get locked in order of response. **Once the lineup hits a multiple of 4, the agent posts to the group thread**: \"We're at 8 — that's two courts. Waitlist now open.\" Anyone confirming after that lands on the waitlist in order.\n\n**The bail handler.** When someone messages \"can't make it\" or doesn't confirm by Friday 6pm, the agent (a) DMs the top of the waitlist privately (\"spot opened for Saturday — you in?\"), (b) updates the lineup card if they say yes, (c) re-posts the updated card to the group thread. The 24-hour rule from the Reddit thread becomes a soft norm the agent enforces gently: under 24 hours, the canceller is asked first if they want to find their own sub before the waitlist promotes.\n\n### 5C. How the agent books courts (capability-scoped handoff)\n\nDirect court reservation systems are not connected. The agent does NOT claim to book. The agent's actual move: web-browse to find court availability at the player's regular location, present 1-3 court options ranked by weather appropriateness, draft the exact booking message (URL or phone number), and hand it to the creator: *\"Court 3 at Memorial Park is open at 9. Here's the booking link — confirm and I'll log it.\"* Once the creator confirms it's done, the agent logs the booking and moves on. **Verifier rule**: if the agent is about to post \"court is booked,\" the message must be gated by a human-confirmation flag set on a prior turn. No flag = no claim.\n\n### 5D. How the agent sends reminders (THE HOOK + THE SCHEDULE)\n\nFour scheduled sends, each gated by the value rule (skip silently if no new substance):\n\n1. **Tuesday RSVP nudge** (DM, per member) — \"We on this Saturday? Last week was 10-2 at Memorial. Same time?\" Skip if member already said yes/no this week. Pause lever: \"Say pause Tuesday pings anytime.\"\n2. **Friday weather + court call** (group post) — fires only if the lineup is at quorum. \"Saturday 9am: 12% rain. Memorial outdoor, courts 3-4. Final RSVPs by 6.\" Skip if already posted.\n3. **Saturday morning lineup + 2-hour ping** (group post) — locked lineup, waitlist visible, court info, two-hour heads-up scheduled.\n4. **Sunday post-game prompt** (DM to creator) — \"Good game. Eric bailed under 24, Sam covered. Want me to start next Saturday at the same time, same court?\" This is the loop-restart move — see 5E.\n\nFirst-firing feedback ask appended to each cadence the first time only: \"first Tuesday nudge — keep this tone, or too direct?\" Drop after.\n\n### 5E. How the agent books the next sync (the loop-restart move — Impulse → Identity)\n\nAfter Saturday's game closes, Pickler doesn't ask \"want to play again?\" — that's open-ended and dies in silence. Instead, on Sunday morning (creator's TZ), Pickler DMs the creator one prefilled prompt: *\"Next Saturday, same time, same court? I'll start Tuesday DMs if you give the nod.\"* One tap → the entire next loop is queued. This is the Spotify continuity move: the next song is already loaded; the user only confirms. If the creator says no or wants a change (different day, different court, paused for a week), the agent updates the schedule and queues the next attempt at the appropriate window. The queue is never empty.\n\n## 6. The Optionality Spec (code first; model only at voice moments)\n\n- **Code:** counting RSVPs to multiples of 4, waitlist ordering, attendance tracking, multiples-of-4 math, schedule firing, value-gate evaluation, formatting the lineup card.\n- **External (Browse / Search):** weather lookup, court availability search, booking URL discovery.\n- **Model call:** the Tuesday DM wording, the Sunday \"next Saturday?\" prompt, the welcome message, the gentle bail-handler phrasing, voice calibration to the group's tone after observing 3-4 weeks.\n- **Human in the loop:** confirming the court booking, approving the invite list when a new player is being added, deciding the 24-hour cancel norm.\n\n## 7. The Continuity Queue (what's always teed up)\n\nAfter every action, the agent updates the queue with the next-best-action and its trigger condition. At any moment, the lineup person can ask \"what's next?\" and Pickler has the answer ready: \"Tuesday I DM the A-team. Friday I check weather and finalize. Saturday morning the card goes out. Sunday I ask about the following week.\" The queue itself is an HTML artifact (`pickler-this-week.html`) the crea", "notes": "Real published template 'Pickler 1' (dev, id 5a337e84). Long (11.5k chars; stored truncated to the 8000 the classifier sees) fully-developed agent design doc. pre-#265 wrongly classified this as design — the case #265 existed to fix. Written largely in THIRD PERSON about 'the agent' with ## markdown headings (no frontmatter / distinctive all-caps headers): completeness, not who-it-addresses, is what should make it passthrough."}
